  "reasoning": "The patient presents with nausea, persistent chest pain, shivering, and anxiety. In clinical symptom profiling, upper gastrointestinal conditions frequently manifest as chest or epigastric pain accompanied by nausea. The prominent mention of anxiety is a key diagnostic indicator, as psychological stress and anxiety are strongly correlated with peptic ulcer disease and often exacerbate its symptoms. Shivering can be attributed to the systemic discomfort and stress response from chronic pain. Other options are less likely: hypertension typically features headaches and dizziness rather than nausea and shivering; GERD focuses more on heartburn, sour taste, and positional worsening; respiratory infections (pneumonia, asthma) would prominently include cough and breathing difficulties; and viral fevers (malaria, dengue, typhoid) would highlight high fever, rash, or specific systemic signs. Therefore, the combination of upper chest/abdominal pain, nausea, and anxiety best aligns with peptic ulcer disease.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]peptic ulcer disease[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}
